{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>That you may live, you and your seed.</b> He mentioned ״וְזַרְעֶךָ״ here because they said in the Midrash: ״הַעִדֹתִי בָכֶם הַיּוֹם אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — look at the sun that I created to serve you, perhaps it has changed its measure, etc.; and these that were not created for reward and not for loss and they do not spare their sons and their daughters — yet they did not change their measure; you, who were created for reward and punishment and you spare your sons and your daughters — how much more so should you not change your ways. Such is the version in the Yalkut, parashat Ha'azinu (32, 962). And whence does the author of this midrash know to frame this a fortiori argument in terms of sons and daughters? Only because he found written after this testimony the verse ״לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה אַתָּה וְזַרְעֶךָ״, and it is difficult for him: why did he mention his seed in this promise more than in all the other promises? Rather, thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: If you do not emerge from the armor of your evil deeds on your own account — for tangible pleasure presses upon you so that you will not feel the future — at least emerge on account of your seed, for they have no share in this pleasure, but in the punishment they do have a share." "And he said: <b>I call heaven and earth to witness against you this day.</b> For when you look at the daily sphere that serves out of love, so too you — ״לְאַהֲבָה אֶת ה׳״, etc., and as it is stated (Judges 5:31): ״וְאֹהֲבָיו כְּצֵאת הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ בִּגְבוּרָתוֹ״. And see the explanation of this entire statement in my small work Orach le-Chayyim, discourse 6."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The idiom אֵינוֹ יוֹצֵא מֵאִצְטַגְנִינוּת, literally 'does not exit the armor/stronghold of,' rendered as 'does not emerge from the armor of.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:58.333198924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}